IDEA PIN (O2 head 3) — WALLED on infra (2026-07-05 sweep), corrected
2026-09-06: the earlier claim that key addressing "as an LVALUE (stored
target) is buildable today" was measured FALSE. `stored { pool[id: 7].hp:
x }` splices the bracket interior verbatim into `__koru_resolve(id: 7)` —
source syntax in a host call position, invalid Zig. The `key:` decl emits
no lookup machinery at all (no key→row map, no maintenance at insert/
take/swap-remove); keyed addressing was never partially working, it is
aspirational syntax with no lowered form.

Honored nowhere ⇒ refused everywhere reachable: 690_349 pins the refusal
(KORU161 on stored targets, stored values, take heads, insert seeds — one
shared gate, `KeyedAddressing` in store.keyed.kz; KORU168 in {{ }}
interpolation across all four engines — which additionally misfired the
format-spec split, taking the bracket ':' as the spec separator).
Consumers of `store[...]` that still lack a refusal channel — the shared
read rewriter storeIndexedRefs (store.kz) reached from rule/query/guard
positions, and `__printInterpolateJs` (the JS print family has no
reporter) — can still emit the verbatim splice; extend the wall when they
get one.

THE CREDITOR — koru-examples/downloads. It runs today with a linear key
scan standing in for this head: an inner `! sweep x when x.idx ==
pr.index` nested inside the batch sweep, purely to reach the one row a
`! progress` names. The scan is the shape this pin retires.

Honoring means emitting a key→row map keyed on the declared column,
maintained at insert/take/swap-remove (swap-remove must update the moved
row's entry — same invariant the position-head pin 690_092 measures).
Reaching for a POSITION instead is not the shortcut it looks like: a
stored position loses its write silently across a removal (690_092). A
declared key is the identity that survives, which is why this head and
that defect are one piece of work.
